--- A list literal holding a list variable of 64-bit ints ([first], first = range[3]),
--- then a list pushed onto it whose items are sums of its own items and the literal's
--- (the dynamic-programming table of an LCS or an edit distance, built row by row).
---
--- Interpreted and compiled alike, before anything runs:
---   longest_common_subsequence-1.co: internal error: the lowered program disagrees
---   with itself — a bug in Crow, not in this program: longest_common_subsequence-1
---   main: line 17: a list of List(Int) holds an item of type List(Big)
--- Expected: [[0, 1, 2], [0, 1, 3]]
---
--- Writing the call in the literal, rows = [range[3]], runs fine in both modes, as
--- does a row whose items read only the row itself or only rows. Related to the open
--- "a list literal holding a list of growing ints beside a list of 64-bit ints", but
--- here the literal holds one list and the growing one arrives by push. Inside a
--- function (first = for j in range[n] / 0) it is the same.
first = range[3]
rows = [first]
row = [0]
j = 1
while j lt 3
    row = push[row, rows[0][j] + row[j - 1]]
    j = j + 1
rows = push[rows, row]
log[rows]
